/* ================= Practice Test 1 · Reading and Writing =================
   Module 1: mixed difficulty. Module 2: an easier (m2e) and a harder (m2h) version.
   Each module: 27 questions, in the official order of question types. */
testSet('t1', 'RW', 'm1', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The new rail line will ______ travel time between the two cities, cutting the trip from three hours to just under one.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['reduce', 'extend', 'measure', 'reverse'], a: 0, x: 'Cutting the trip from three hours to one means the line will **reduce** travel time.', w: ['', 'Cutting the trip from three hours to one shortens travel time; it doesn’t extend it.', 'The line changes travel time; it doesn’t just measure it.', '“Reverse” makes no sense for travel time; the trip gets shorter.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'Unlike her colleagues, who favored bold experiments, the chemist was ______: she tested every assumption twice before moving on to the next step.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['reckless', 'cautious', 'indifferent', 'impatient'], a: 1, x: 'Testing every assumption twice is the opposite of “bold”, so she was **cautious**.', w: ['“Reckless” describes her bold colleagues, not someone who tests everything twice.', '', 'Testing every assumption twice shows she cares a great deal, not that she’s indifferent.', 'Checking everything twice before moving on is the opposite of impatient.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The economist’s argument **rests on** the assumption that consumers always act in their own financial interest; if that assumption is false, much of the argument collapses.', p: 'As used in the text, what does the phrase “rests on” most nearly mean?', o: ['relaxes on', 'stops at', 'depends on', 'sleeps through'], a: 2, x: 'If the assumption fails, the argument collapses, so the argument **depends on** it.', w: ['An argument can’t relax; “rests on” here means relies on.', 'The argument doesn’t stop at the assumption; it needs it to stand up.', '', '“Sleeps through” is a joke meaning of “rest”; it doesn’t fit an argument.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'Although the medieval manuscript was badly water-damaged, scholars were able to ______ most of its text by comparing the surviving pages with two later copies.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['obscure', 'abbreviate', 'dismiss', 'reconstruct'], a: 3, x: 'Comparing damaged pages with copies lets scholars rebuild, or **reconstruct**, the missing text.', w: ['Scholars made the text readable again, the opposite of obscuring it.', 'Nothing suggests they shortened the text; they recovered it.', 'They worked hard to recover the text, so they didn’t dismiss it.', ''] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 2, ps: 'Gray wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in 1995. ==In the years that followed, elk began avoiding open river valleys, where they were easy for wolves to spot.== With less grazing along the rivers, willow and aspen trees grew back in many places.', p: Q_FUNC, o: ['It describes a change in elk behavior that helps explain the regrowth of trees described later.', 'It argues that elk should be removed from the park.', 'It explains why wolves were first removed from Yellowstone.', 'It compares the diets of wolves and elk.'], a: 0, x: 'The elk’s new behavior (avoiding valleys) leads to less grazing, which explains why trees grew back.', w: ['', 'The text doesn’t argue for removing elk.', 'The text doesn’t explain why wolves were removed.', 'Diets aren’t compared; the sentence is about where elk went.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 1, ps: 'Many people assume that tomatoes have always been part of Italian cooking. In fact, tomatoes are native to the Americas and did not reach Europe until the 1500s. For many years afterward, some Europeans grew them only as decorative plants.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To explain how to grow tomatoes in Italy', 'To argue that Italian cooking should not use tomatoes', 'To correct a common belief about the history of tomatoes in European cooking', 'To compare tomatoes with other plants from the Americas'], a: 2, x: 'The text sets up a common assumption (“always part of Italian cooking”) and then corrects it with history.', w: ['The text gives no growing instructions.', 'The text doesn’t say Italian cooking shouldn’t use tomatoes.', '', 'No other plants from the Americas are discussed.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 3, ps: '**Text 1**\nUranus is tipped on its side, spinning at an angle of about 98 degrees. Some astronomers argue that a single giant collision early in the planet’s history knocked it over.\n\n**Text 2**\nComputer simulations by one research team suggest that a single impact strong enough to tip Uranus so far would also have disrupted the orbits of its moons, which today circle neatly around its tilted equator. The team proposes instead that two or more smaller impacts tilted the planet in stages.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the research team in Text 2 most likely respond to the argument in Text 1?', o: ['By agreeing that a single collision best explains the tilt', 'By arguing that Uranus was never struck by any objects', 'By suggesting that a single collision is unlikely to explain the tilt, given the orderly orbits of the planet’s moons', 'By claiming that Uranus’s moons formed long after the planet tilted'], a: 2, x: 'Text 2 says one big impact would have disrupted the moons’ orbits, so it doubts the single-collision explanation and proposes several smaller impacts.', w: ['Text 2’s team doubts the single-collision explanation.', 'Text 2 proposes two or more impacts, so Uranus was struck.', '', 'Text 2 says nothing about when the moons formed.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 1, ps: 'In 1816, the French doctor René Laennec needed to listen to a patient’s heart but found it awkward to press his ear to her chest. He rolled a sheet of paper into a tube, placed one end on her chest and the other at his ear, and found that the heartbeat sounded clearer than ever. He soon built a wooden version of the tube, which he called a stethoscope.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['Laennec invented the stethoscope as a better way to listen to a patient’s chest.', 'Laennec was the first doctor to study the human heart.', 'Paper tubes work better than wooden ones for listening to the heart.', 'Doctors in 1816 rarely examined their patients.'], a: 0, x: 'The text describes how Laennec came up with the stethoscope to hear a patient’s heart more clearly.', w: ['', 'The text never says he was the first to study the heart.', 'Laennec built the wooden version after the paper tube; the text doesn’t say paper was better.', 'The text is about Laennec examining a patient, not about how often doctors did.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 3, ps: 'Grandmother Ines never threw anything away. A kitchen drawer held rubber bands saved from forty years of newspapers, and her hall closet held every coat she had ever owned. Yet when her grandson asked about the old violin in the attic, she brought it down at once and handed it to him, saying only that it was “about time someone played it.”', p: 'Based on the text, what is most strongly suggested about Ines?', o: ['She no longer enjoys music.', 'She wants to clear out her attic.', 'She does not know who owned the violin.', 'She values the violin being played more than keeping it for herself.'], a: 3, x: 'Ines keeps everything, so giving the violin away is unusual; her comment shows she wants it to be used.', w: ['Nothing suggests she dislikes music; she wants the violin played.', 'She keeps everything else, so she isn’t trying to clear out space.', 'Nothing suggests she doesn’t know whose violin it was.', ''] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 2, ps: 'In a poem, the speaker expresses admiration for the persistence of a desert river.', p: 'Which quotation from the poem most effectively illustrates this claim?', o: ['“The river runs beneath a sky of brass.”', '“On its banks, the willows bend and sway.”', '“Through stone and drought it finds its way, / and cuts the canyon, day by day.”', '“At dusk, the herons gather in the shallows.”'], a: 2, x: 'Finding its way through stone and drought, day after day, shows the river’s persistence. The other lines describe the scene, not persistence.', w: ['A brass-colored sky describes the scene, not the river’s persistence.', 'Willows on the banks describe the scene, not the river.', '', 'Herons gathering describes the scene, not the river’s persistence.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'Town officials claim that the new protected bike lanes on Grant Avenue caused the drop in traffic accidents there over the past year.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the officials’ claim?', o: ['More people rode bikes on Grant Avenue after the lanes opened.', 'Accidents fell by a similar amount on nearby streets that did not get bike lanes.', 'The bike lanes cost less to build than expected.', 'Grant Avenue is one of the busiest streets in town.'], a: 1, x: 'If accidents fell just as much without bike lanes, something else probably caused the drop.', w: ['More riders doesn’t say what caused the drop in accidents.', '', 'Cost has nothing to do with whether the lanes reduced accidents.', 'How busy the street is doesn’t test whether the lanes caused the drop.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 2, ps: 'After a wildfire, ecologists measured how many trees of three species showed new growth in two burned areas. They concluded that oaks recovered better than pines in both areas: ______', ch: { t: 'table', title: 'Trees showing new growth one year after the fire', head: ['Species', 'Area A', 'Area B'], rows: [['Oak', '72%', '65%'], ['Pine', '41%', '38%'], ['Maple', '55%', '60%']] }, p: Q_DATA, o: ['in Area B, 60% of maples showed new growth.', 'in Area A, 72% of oaks showed new growth, compared with 41% of pines, and in Area B, the figures were 65% and 38%.', 'in Area A, 41% of pines showed new growth.', 'maples showed more new growth in Area B than in Area A.'], a: 1, x: 'The conclusion compares oaks and pines in both areas; only this choice does that.', w: ['Maples aren’t part of the comparison between oaks and pines.', '', 'This gives only pines in one area, so nothing is compared.', 'This compares maples in two areas, not oaks and pines.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 1, ps: 'Penguins cannot fly, but their wings are stiff, flat flippers that they beat to “fly” through the water, reaching speeds of several meters per second while chasing fish. This suggests that penguins’ wings are ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['adapted for swimming rather than for flying.', 'too weak to be used at all.', 'mainly used to keep penguins warm.', 'identical to the wings of flying birds.'], a: 0, x: 'The wings are flippers used to move fast underwater, so they’re adapted for swimming.', w: ['', 'The flippers drive penguins through the water at high speed, so they aren’t too weak to use.', 'The text describes the wings as used for swimming, not for warmth.', 'Flying birds’ wings let them fly; penguins’ stiff flippers don’t.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'A historian notes that a river town’s 1850 census lists 40 blacksmiths and 3 machinists, while its 1880 census lists 6 blacksmiths and 51 machinists. Over the same period, a railroad repair shop opened in the town. The historian concludes that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the town’s population fell sharply after 1850.', 'blacksmiths in the town earned more money in 1880 than in 1850.', 'the town’s work likely shifted from traditional metalwork toward machinery during those decades.', 'the railroad repair shop closed before 1880.'], a: 2, x: 'Fewer blacksmiths, many more machinists and a new repair shop all point to a shift toward machine work.', w: ['The census counts don’t show total population.', 'Earnings aren’t mentioned.', '', 'Machinists increased, which fits the repair shop still operating.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'The robot vacuum maps each room before it ______ it rarely bumps into furniture.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['cleans so,', 'cleans, so', 'cleans, so,', 'cleans; and so,'], a: 1, x: 'A comma plus “so” joins the two complete sentences. No comma goes after “so”.', w: ['The comma belongs before “so”, not after it.', '', 'No comma belongs after “so”.', 'A semicolon shouldn’t be followed by “and so”, and no comma belongs after it.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'Marie Tharp, a geologist and ______ created some of the first detailed maps of the Atlantic Ocean floor in the 1950s.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['cartographer', 'cartographer;', 'cartographer:', 'cartographer,'], a: 3, x: 'The description “a geologist and cartographer” is set off by commas; the opening comma needs a closing one.', w: ['The description opens with a comma, so it needs a closing comma before “created”.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “created”.', 'A colon would cut the subject off from its verb, “created”.', ''] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'This year, the summer festival features three kinds of ______ classical, jazz, and folk.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['music;', 'music:', 'music, such as:', 'music'], a: 1, x: 'A complete sentence introduces the list, so a colon fits. “Such as:” is wrong.', w: ['A semicolon can’t introduce a list.', '', 'A colon can’t follow “such as”.', 'Without punctuation, “music classical” runs together.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The oldest known cave paintings of animals are on the Indonesian island of ______ one of them, a painting of a wild pig, is at least 45,000 years old.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['Sulawesi,', 'Sulawesi', 'Sulawesi;', 'Sulawesi, which'], a: 2, x: 'Two complete sentences need a semicolon (or a period). A comma alone is a comma splice.', w: ['A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', '', '“Which” makes the second part a clause about Sulawesi, and “one of them” no longer fits the sentence.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'Each evening in autumn, a flock of thousands of starlings ______ across the sky in a swirling cloud.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['move', 'moves', 'are moving', 'have moved'], a: 1, x: 'The subject is “a flock” (singular), so the verb is “moves”.', w: ['The subject is “a flock”, which is singular.', '', '“Are” is plural, but “a flock” is singular.', '“Have” is plural, but “a flock” is singular.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'Neither the coach nor the players ______ expecting the storm to delay the championship match.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['was', 'is', 'were', 'has been'], a: 2, x: 'With “neither… nor”, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, “players” (plural).', w: ['With “neither… nor”, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, “players”, which is plural.', 'With “neither… nor”, the verb agrees with “players”, which is plural, and the sentence is in the past.', '', '“Has” is singular, but the verb must agree with “players”.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'Having studied the ruins for more than a decade, ______', p: Q_SEC, o: ['a new date for the city’s founding was proposed by the archaeologist.', 'the archaeologist proposed a new date for the city’s founding.', 'the city’s founding was given a new date by the archaeologist.', 'there was a new date proposed for the city’s founding.'], a: 1, x: 'The person who studied the ruins is the archaeologist, who must come right after the comma.', w: ['This makes it sound like the new date studied the ruins.', '', 'This makes it sound like the city’s founding studied the ruins.', 'This leaves no one who studied the ruins.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'Hurricanes draw their energy from warm ocean water. ______ they usually weaken quickly once they move over land, where that energy source is missing.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['For example,', 'Consequently,', 'Similarly,', 'In contrast,'], a: 1, x: 'Hurricanes weaken over land **because** they lose their energy source: a cause-and-effect relationship.', w: ['Weakening over land isn’t an example of drawing energy from warm water; it’s a result.', '', 'Weakening over land isn’t similar to drawing energy from water; it follows from it.', 'The second sentence follows from the first, so there’s no contrast.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'The museum’s collection includes thousands of paintings from six centuries. ______ its most visited room holds just one work: a small portrait painted in 1665.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['However,', 'Therefore,', 'For instance,', 'Likewise,'], a: 0, x: 'A huge collection contrasts with the most popular room holding a single work.', w: ['', 'One popular painting isn’t a result of having thousands of paintings.', 'One room with one painting isn’t an example of a huge collection; it contrasts with it.', 'One painting isn’t similar to thousands; it’s a contrast.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Maps of the region from the 1700s showed a large lake in its center. ______ later surveys found only a series of seasonal marshes that flood during wet years.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Similarly,', 'Thus,', 'Additionally,', 'In reality,'], a: 3, x: 'The surveys reveal what was actually there, correcting the old maps: “In reality”.', w: ['The surveys found something different from the maps, not something similar.', 'Finding marshes isn’t a result of the old maps.', 'The surveys correct the maps rather than adding to them.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- The okapi lives in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo.\n- It has striped legs that resemble a zebra’s.\n- Its closest living relative is the giraffe.\n- It was first described by European scientists in 1901.', p: 'The student wants to identify the animal the okapi is most closely related to. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The okapi has striped legs that resemble a zebra’s.', 'The okapi lives in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo.', 'The okapi was described by European scientists in 1901.', 'The okapi’s closest living relative is the giraffe.'], a: 3, x: 'Only one choice names the okapi’s closest relative. The zebra comparison is about appearance, not relationship.', w: ['This compares the okapi’s appearance to a zebra; it doesn’t name its closest relative.', 'This tells where it lives, not what it’s related to.', 'This gives a date, not its closest relative.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- Emily Dickinson (1830–1886) wrote nearly 1,800 poems.\n- Only about ten of her poems were published while she was alive.\n- Franz Kafka (1883–1924) asked a friend to burn his unpublished manuscripts after his death.\n- The friend published them instead, including the novels *The Trial* and *The Castle*.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Dickinson and Kafka. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Dickinson wrote nearly 1,800 poems, while Kafka wrote novels.', 'Both Dickinson and Kafka had most of their work published only after their deaths.', 'Kafka asked a friend to burn his manuscripts.', 'Dickinson lived from 1830 to 1886, and Kafka lived from 1883 to 1924.'], a: 1, x: 'The notes show that both writers’ work reached readers mostly after they died. “Dickinson wrote poems, while Kafka wrote novels” is a difference, not a similarity.', w: ['This points out a difference, not a similarity.', '', 'This is about Kafka only.', 'This gives their life dates, which isn’t a meaningful similarity.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- Researchers wanted to know whether playing music to plants affects their growth.\n- They grew 60 bean plants under identical light, water and temperature.\n- Half the plants heard music for three hours a day; half did not.\n- After six weeks, there was no significant difference in the plants’ height or weight.', p: 'The student wants to present the study and its conclusion to an audience unfamiliar with it. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Researchers grew 60 bean plants under identical light, water and temperature.', 'After six weeks, the plants did not differ significantly in height or weight.', 'In a study comparing bean plants grown with and without daily music under otherwise identical conditions, researchers found no significant difference in growth.', 'Some plants heard music for three hours a day.'], a: 2, x: 'An unfamiliar audience needs both what was tested and what was found. Only one choice gives both.', w: ['This gives the setup without the conclusion.', 'This gives the result without explaining what was tested.', '', 'This gives one detail without the study’s purpose or conclusion.'] }
]);
testSet('t1', 'RW', 'm2e', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The library stayed open late during exam week so that students would have more time to ______ for their tests.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['sleep', 'study', 'travel', 'complain'], a: 1, x: 'Students need extra time to prepare for exams: to **study**.', w: ['Sleeping doesn’t require the library to stay open.', '', 'Students don’t need a library open late to travel.', 'Nothing suggests students needed time to complain.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'After six weeks without rain, the farmers were ______ to see dark clouds gathering over the hills.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['relieved', 'annoyed', 'confused', 'bored'], a: 0, x: 'After a long dry spell, rain clouds would make farmers **relieved**.', w: ['', 'Farmers waiting six weeks for rain would be glad, not annoyed.', 'Rain clouds after a drought aren’t confusing.', 'Rain after a long drought is welcome news, not boring.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The physicist’s explanation was so ______ that even audience members with no science background could follow every step.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['technical', 'tedious', 'lucid', 'hesitant'], a: 2, x: 'If anyone could follow it, the explanation was clear: **lucid**.', w: ['A technical explanation would be hard for people with no science background.', '“Tedious” means boring, which doesn’t explain why everyone could follow it.', '', 'A hesitant explanation wouldn’t be easy to follow.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'Before the final game of the season, the coach asked everyone in the stadium to **observe** a moment of silence for a former player.', p: Q_MEAN('observe'), o: ['watch', 'notice', 'study', 'keep'], a: 3, x: 'To “observe a moment of silence” means to **keep** it, not to watch or study it.', w: ['You don’t watch a moment of silence; you take part in it.', '“Notice” doesn’t fit; the crowd is asked to take part in the silence.', 'A moment of silence isn’t something to study.', ''] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 1, ps: 'The town’s lantern festival began in 1952 as a small gathering of neighbors on the riverbank. Today it draws more than 20,000 visitors, who release paper lanterns onto the water at dusk.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To explain how paper lanterns are made', 'To describe how a local festival has grown over time', 'To argue that the festival should be moved', 'To compare two different festivals'], a: 1, x: 'The text moves from a small 1952 gathering to a festival with 20,000 visitors: it shows growth.', w: ['The text doesn’t explain how lanterns are made.', '', 'The text doesn’t argue for moving the festival.', 'Only one festival is described.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 2, ps: 'Many people believe that lightning never strikes the same place twice. Tall structures, however, are struck again and again: the Empire State Building in New York City is hit by lightning about 20 to 25 times a year.', p: Q_STRUCT, o: ['It states a common belief and then gives an example that contradicts it.', 'It explains how lightning forms and then describes a storm.', 'It compares two famous buildings.', 'It describes a problem and then proposes a solution.'], a: 0, x: 'The text presents the belief, then the Empire State Building example shows it’s false.', w: ['', 'The text doesn’t explain how lightning forms.', 'Only one building is described.', 'No problem or solution is presented.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 2, ps: '**Text 1**\nBreakfast is the most important meal of the day. People who eat breakfast are healthier and more focused than people who skip it.\n\n**Text 2**\nStudies comparing breakfast eaters with breakfast skippers have found mixed results. Some researchers think that what people eat for breakfast (for example, oatmeal or a sugary pastry) matters more than whether they eat it at all.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the researchers mentioned in Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?', o: ['They would agree that every breakfast improves health.', 'They would suggest that the claim is too simple, since the effects may depend on the kind of breakfast.', 'They would argue that people should never eat breakfast.', 'They would claim that breakfast has no effect on anyone.'], a: 1, x: 'Text 2 reports mixed results and says the type of breakfast matters, which qualifies Text 1’s broad claim.', w: ['Text 2 reports mixed results, so it wouldn’t say every breakfast helps.', '', 'Text 2 doesn’t say people should skip breakfast.', 'Text 2 says the type of breakfast may matter, so it doesn’t say breakfast has no effect.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 1, ps: 'Many desert plants have leaves covered in a thick, waxy coating. The wax slows the loss of water from the leaves into the hot, dry air, which helps the plants survive long periods without rain.', p: 'According to the text, why do some desert plants have waxy leaves?', o: ['To attract insects that pollinate them', 'To reflect moonlight at night', 'To reduce the amount of water they lose', 'To make the leaves taste bad to animals'], a: 2, x: 'The text says the wax “slows the loss of water”.', w: ['Insects aren’t mentioned.', 'Moonlight isn’t mentioned.', '', 'Taste and animals aren’t mentioned.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'The Rosetta Stone, found in Egypt in 1799, carries the same official decree written three ways: in Egyptian hieroglyphs, in a later Egyptian script called Demotic, and in ancient Greek. Because scholars could already read Greek, they could compare the three versions. Using the stone, the French scholar Jean-François Champollion announced in 1822 that he had deciphered hieroglyphs.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['The Rosetta Stone helped scholars decipher hieroglyphs because it contained the same text in three scripts.', 'The Rosetta Stone was found by Jean-François Champollion.', 'Ancient Greek is easier to read than hieroglyphs.', 'Demotic was the oldest form of Egyptian writing.'], a: 0, x: 'The text explains why the stone mattered: the same decree in a known language (Greek) and in hieroglyphs.', w: ['', 'The text doesn’t say who found the stone; Champollion used it to decipher hieroglyphs.', 'The text says scholars could already read Greek, not that Greek is easier in general.', 'Demotic is described as a later script, not the oldest.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 1, ps: 'In a short story, the narrator is nervous before her first piano recital.', p: 'Which quotation from the story most effectively illustrates this claim?', o: ['“The concert hall had red velvet seats.”', '“My teacher wore a blue scarf that evening.”', '“My hands were trembling so badly that I nearly dropped my sheet music.”', '“The recital began at seven o’clock.”'], a: 2, x: 'Trembling hands show nervousness; the other quotations describe the setting.', w: ['Red seats describe the setting, not her feelings.', 'Her teacher’s scarf describes the setting, not her nerves.', '', 'A start time is a fact, not a sign of nervousness.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 2, ps: 'A gardener hypothesizes that adding used coffee grounds to the soil helps tomato plants grow.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the gardener’s hypothesis?', o: ['Coffee grounds are easy to collect from cafés.', 'Tomato plants need plenty of sunlight.', 'Some gardeners prefer to grow tomatoes in containers.', 'Tomato plants given coffee grounds grew taller than otherwise identical plants that were not.'], a: 3, x: 'Comparing otherwise identical plants isolates the effect of the coffee grounds.', w: ['How easy the grounds are to collect says nothing about plant growth.', 'Sunlight needs don’t test the effect of coffee grounds.', 'Container gardening has nothing to do with coffee grounds.', ''] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 1, ps: 'Four friends kept track of how many books they read over the summer. Marco read the most books of the four: ______', ch: { t: 'bar', title: 'Books read over the summer', x: ['Ana', 'Marco', 'Lee', 'Jada'], s: [{ n: 'Books', v: [6, 11, 8, 9] }] }, p: Q_GRAPH, o: ['Ana read 6 books.', 'Lee and Jada read a total of 17 books.', 'Marco read 11 books, more than any of the other three friends.', 'Jada read more books than Lee.'], a: 2, x: 'Only one choice compares Marco’s total with everyone else’s.', w: ['This gives only Ana’s total, without showing Marco read the most.', 'This combines Lee and Jada but doesn’t compare anyone with Marco.', '', 'This compares Jada and Lee, not Marco.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 1, ps: 'A cat’s whiskers are very sensitive to touch and grow to about the width of its body. When a cat pokes its head into a narrow gap, the whiskers help it judge ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['whether its body will fit through the opening.', 'how hungry it is.', 'what color the gap is.', 'how far away its owner is.'], a: 0, x: 'Whiskers as wide as the body that touch the sides of a gap would tell the cat whether it fits.', w: ['', 'Whiskers that touch the sides of a gap don’t tell the cat how hungry it is.', 'Whiskers sense touch, not color.', 'The whiskers are poking into a gap, not sensing the owner.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 2, ps: 'A café noticed that sales of iced drinks rose sharply on days above 30°C and fell on cooler days. The weather service has forecast a week of temperatures above 32°C. To prepare, the café should probably ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['stop selling hot drinks entirely.', 'order extra ice and ingredients for iced drinks.', 'close for the week.', 'lower the price of iced drinks to sell more.'], a: 1, x: 'Hot days mean more iced-drink sales, so stocking up is the logical step.', w: ['Hot drinks may still sell; nothing suggests stopping them entirely.', '', 'Hot days are when iced drinks sell best, so closing would lose sales.', 'Sales already rise on hot days; lowering prices isn’t needed to prepare.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'The city marathon begins at 7:00 ______ are asked to arrive at least an hour early.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['a.m., runners', 'a.m.; runners', 'a.m. runners', 'a.m., and, runners'], a: 1, x: 'Two complete sentences are joined, so a semicolon works. A comma alone is a comma splice.', w: ['A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', '', 'With no punctuation, “a.m. runners” runs the two sentences together.', 'No comma belongs after “and”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'My three favorite foods ______ pizza, tacos, and curry.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['are:', 'are;', 'are', 'are,'], a: 2, x: 'The verb “are” leads straight into the list, so no punctuation is needed.', w: ['A colon can’t come right after a verb like “are”; the verb leads straight into the list.', 'A semicolon can’t introduce a list.', '', 'A comma would separate the verb “are” from its complement.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The Great Barrier Reef, which stretches for more than 2,000 ______ is the world’s largest coral reef system.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['kilometers', 'kilometers,', 'kilometers;', 'kilometers —'], a: 1, x: 'The “which” clause was opened with a comma, so it must be closed with one.', w: ['The “which” clause opens with a comma, so it needs a closing comma.', '', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “is”.', 'The clause opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma, not a dash.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'Before the invention of the electric ______ people kept food cold with blocks of ice cut from frozen lakes.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['refrigerator', 'refrigerator;', 'refrigerator,', 'refrigerator:'], a: 2, x: 'An introductory phrase (“Before the invention…”) is followed by a comma.', w: ['An introductory phrase needs a comma before the main clause.', 'A semicolon needs a complete sentence before it.', '', 'A colon needs a complete sentence before it.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'Every student in the three biology classes ______ a signed permission slip before the field trip.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['need', 'needs', 'are needing', 'have needed'], a: 1, x: '“Every student” is singular, so the verb is “needs”.', w: ['“Every student” is singular, so the verb must be singular.', '', '“Are” is plural, but “every student” is singular.', '“Have” is plural, but “every student” is singular.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'The science museum opened ______ new planetarium last spring.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['it’s', 'their', 'its', 'its’'], a: 2, x: 'The planetarium belongs to the museum (singular), so the possessive is “its”.', w: ['“It’s” means “it is”.', 'The museum is singular, so “their” doesn’t agree.', '', '“Its’” isn’t a word.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'Last summer, the twins ______ a lemonade stand and donated the profits to an animal shelter.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['run', 'runs', 'ran', 'have ran'], a: 2, x: '“Last summer” is in the past, so the simple past “ran” is right. “Have ran” is not a correct form.', w: ['“Run” is present tense, but “last summer” is in the past.', '“Runs” is present tense and singular, but “twins” is plural and the time is past.', '', '“Have ran” isn’t a correct verb form; it would be “have run”.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'Maria practiced the piano every day for a year. ______ she won first prize at the regional competition.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['However,', 'Eventually,', 'For example,', 'Instead,'], a: 1, x: 'The prize came after a long period of practice, so a time transition fits.', w: ['Winning the prize doesn’t contrast with practicing; it follows from it.', '', 'The prize isn’t an example of practicing.', '“Instead” signals a replacement, but the prize came after the practice.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'Bananas are a good source of potassium. ______ they contain vitamin B6 and fiber.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['In contrast,', 'Therefore,', 'In addition,', 'Instead,'], a: 2, x: 'The second sentence adds more nutrients: an addition transition.', w: ['The second sentence adds more nutrients; it doesn’t contrast with potassium.', 'The vitamins aren’t a result of the potassium.', '', '“Instead” signals a replacement, but the second sentence adds more benefits.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'The original recipe calls for butter. ______ cooks who avoid dairy can use olive oil instead.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Therefore,', 'For example,', 'Similarly,', 'However,'], a: 3, x: 'Using olive oil is an alternative that contrasts with the original recipe.', w: ['Using olive oil isn’t a result of the recipe calling for butter.', 'Olive oil isn’t an example of butter; it replaces it.', 'Olive oil is an alternative, not a similar idea.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- Kīlauea is a volcano on the island of Hawaiʻi.\n- It is one of the most active volcanoes in the world.\n- It erupted almost continuously from 1983 to 2018.\n- Its lava flows have added new land to the island.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize how active Kīlauea is. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Kīlauea is a volcano on the island of Hawaiʻi.', 'Lava flows from Kīlauea have added new land to the island.', 'Kīlauea, one of the world’s most active volcanoes, erupted almost continuously from 1983 to 2018.', 'Hawaiʻi has several volcanoes.'], a: 2, x: 'Only one choice highlights Kīlauea’s activity, with its 35 years of near-constant eruption.', w: ['This gives the location, not how active it is.', 'New land shows an effect of eruptions, but not how often it erupts.', '', 'This is about Hawaiʻi, not Kīlauea’s activity.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- A soccer team has 11 players on the field at a time.\n- A basketball team has 5 players on the court at a time.\n- Both sports are played around the world.', p: 'The student wants to contrast the number of players in the two sports. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Soccer and basketball are played around the world.', 'A soccer team has 11 players on the field, while a basketball team has only 5 on the court.', 'Basketball is played on a court.', 'A soccer team has 11 players on the field.'], a: 1, x: 'Only one choice compares the two numbers.', w: ['This is a similarity, not a contrast in numbers.', '', 'This gives where basketball is played, not the number of players.', 'This gives only soccer’s number, so nothing is contrasted.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- The Statue of Liberty was a gift from France to the United States.\n- It was dedicated in 1886.\n- Its outer surface is made of copper.\n- Over time, the copper reacted with the air and developed a green layer called a patina.', p: 'The student wants to explain why the Statue of Liberty is green. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The Statue of Liberty was a gift from France to the United States.', 'The statue was dedicated in 1886.', 'The statue’s copper surface reacted with the air over time, forming a green layer called a patina.', 'The statue’s outer surface is made of copper.'], a: 2, x: 'Only one choice gives the cause of the color: the copper reacting with air to form a patina.', w: ['This explains where the statue came from, not why it’s green.', 'A date doesn’t explain the color.', '', 'This gives the material but not the reaction that made it green.'] }
]);
testSet('t1', 'RW', 'm2h', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The critic praised the novel’s ______ portrayal of grief, noting that it avoided both sentimentality and coldness.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['exuberant', 'measured', 'callous', 'perfunctory'], a: 1, x: 'Avoiding both excess emotion and coldness is a balanced, **measured** portrayal.', w: ['“Exuberant” means full of excitement, which would be sentimental, not balanced.', '', '“Callous” means cold and unfeeling, which the portrayal avoided.', '“Perfunctory” means careless and without feeling, which doesn’t earn praise for avoiding coldness.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The experiment’s results were greeted with enthusiasm, but attempts to replicate them proved ______: some laboratories reproduced the effect, while others could not.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['unanimous', 'futile', 'inconsistent', 'effortless'], a: 2, x: 'Some labs succeeded and some didn’t, so the results were **inconsistent**. “Futile” would mean every attempt failed.', w: ['Some labs succeeded and some didn’t, so the results weren’t unanimous.', '“Futile” would mean every attempt failed, but some labs reproduced the effect.', '', 'Nothing suggests the attempts were easy.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The senator’s speech was intended to **temper** public alarm about the new policy, reassuring voters that its effects would be gradual.', p: Q_MEAN('temper'), o: ['moderate', 'harden', 'provoke', 'measure'], a: 0, x: 'Reassuring voters calms their alarm, so “temper” means **moderate**.', w: ['', 'Reassuring voters calms alarm; it doesn’t harden it.', 'Reassurance is meant to calm alarm, not stir it up.', 'The speech was meant to calm alarm, not to measure it.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'Rather than simply ______ earlier theories of motion, the physicist built on them, showing that they were special cases of a broader principle.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['refining', 'endorsing', 'citing', 'discarding'], a: 3, x: '“Rather than… built on them” requires the opposite of building on: **discarding**. “Refining” would be a way of building on them.', w: ['Refining is a way of building on theories, so it doesn’t contrast with “built on them”.', 'Endorsing is close to building on them, so it doesn’t contrast.', 'Citing theories isn’t the opposite of building on them.', ''] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'Biologists once assumed that plants communicate only through chemicals released into the air. ==Recent experiments, however, show that some plants also send signals through networks of fungi that link their roots underground.== These networks can carry chemical warnings about insect attacks from one plant to its neighbors, which then boost their own defenses.', p: Q_FUNC, o: ['It introduces a finding that complicates an earlier assumption and that the rest of the text develops.', 'It summarizes the evidence for the assumption described in the first sentence.', 'It explains why insects attack some plants but not others.', 'It describes a method for growing fungi in a laboratory.'], a: 0, x: 'The underlined sentence contradicts the “only through the air” assumption, and the next sentence explains the fungal networks further.', w: ['', 'The sentence challenges the assumption; it doesn’t support it.', 'The text doesn’t explain why insects choose certain plants.', 'The fungi grow naturally underground; no lab method is described.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 2, ps: 'At first, Kenji thought the city was nothing but noise: horns, sirens and the constant rumble of trains. By the end of his first month, he could tell the 7:10 train from the 7:25 by sound alone, and the honking taxis below his window had become a kind of music.', p: Q_STRUCT, o: ['It describes a character’s first impression of a place and then shows how that impression changed.', 'It compares two cities that a character has lived in.', 'It explains how the city’s train schedule works.', 'It describes a problem that a character fails to solve.'], a: 0, x: 'Kenji goes from hearing only noise to hearing something like music: his impression changes.', w: ['', 'Only one city is described.', 'The train times show Kenji’s familiarity with the city; the schedule isn’t explained.', 'Kenji grows used to the noise, so the text doesn’t describe a failure.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 3, ps: '**Text 1**\nIn a lab study, crows that were shown a tool, and then later a box that required that tool to open, carried the tool with them when they were moved to a new room. The researchers argue that the crows were planning for a future need.\n\n**Text 2**\nA second team cautions that carrying a tool could simply be a learned habit: crows rewarded for keeping tools nearby may keep doing so without imagining any future task. They propose testing crows with a task they have never encountered before.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the second team (Text 2) most likely respond to the argument in Text 1?', o: ['By agreeing that the crows clearly planned ahead', 'By arguing that crows cannot use tools at all', 'By suggesting that the behavior in Text 1 may not show planning unless crows also succeed with an unfamiliar future task', 'By claiming that crows learn faster than other birds'], a: 2, x: 'Text 2 offers another explanation (habit) and proposes a test with a new task, so it doubts that Text 1’s evidence proves planning.', w: ['Text 2 cautions that the behavior could be a habit, so it doesn’t agree it was clearly planning.', 'Text 2 says crows carry tools, so it doesn’t deny that crows use them.', '', 'Text 2 doesn’t compare crows with other birds.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 3, ps: 'Artificial light at night hides all but the brightest stars from most city dwellers. It can also disrupt wildlife: newly hatched sea turtles, for example, may crawl toward streetlights instead of the ocean. Some regions have responded by creating “dark sky reserves,” where outdoor lights must be shielded and dimmed. These areas have become destinations for stargazers, and in some of them, wildlife that depends on darkness has begun to return.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['Sea turtles are the animals most affected by artificial light.', 'Dark sky reserves limit artificial light, which benefits both stargazing and wildlife.', 'All cities should turn off their streetlights at night.', 'Stars are no longer visible anywhere near cities.'], a: 1, x: 'The text describes the problem of light pollution and how dark sky reserves help both stargazers and wildlife.', w: ['Sea turtles are one example; the text doesn’t rank which animals are most affected.', '', 'The text describes dimmed, shielded lights in some regions; it doesn’t say all cities should turn lights off.', 'The text says light hides all but the brightest stars, so some are still visible.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'The letter arrived in March. Ana read it twice, folded it into a small square and tucked it into her coat pocket. For the rest of the day, she smiled at strangers, hummed while she washed the dishes, and twice nearly told her brother about it before stopping herself.', p: 'Based on the text, what is most strongly suggested about the letter?', o: ['It contained bad news that Ana wanted to forget.', 'It contained good news that Ana wanted to keep to herself for now.', 'It was addressed to Ana’s brother.', 'It was written by Ana herself.'], a: 1, x: 'Her smiling and humming suggest good news; stopping herself from telling her brother shows she’s keeping it private.', w: ['She smiles and hums, which suggests good news, not bad.', '', 'She carries the letter herself and nearly tells her brother about it, so it was hers.', 'The letter arrived by mail, so someone else wrote it.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'A historian argues that the spread of radio in the 1920s changed how many Americans experienced professional sports.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian’s argument?', o: ['Several popular radio programs in the 1920s were comedies.', 'By the late 1920s, fans in many cities could hear live broadcasts of major baseball games, which most fans had previously followed only through newspaper reports the next day.', 'Baseball was already popular in the United States before 1920.', 'Radios in the 1920s were expensive and large.'], a: 1, x: 'Hearing games live instead of reading about them later is a direct change in how fans experienced sports.', w: ['Comedy programs don’t show a change in how people experienced sports.', '', 'Baseball being popular before 1920 doesn’t show that radio changed anything.', 'Size and price don’t show how the experience of sports changed.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'After a major hurricane, ecologists found that lizards on a Caribbean island had larger toe pads on average than before the storm. They propose that the hurricane itself caused this change, because lizards with larger toe pads were better able to cling to branches during the high winds and so were more likely to survive.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the ecologists’ proposal?', o: ['On nearby islands that were not hit by the hurricane, lizards’ toe pads grew just as much over the same period.', 'Lizards with larger toe pads can climb smooth surfaces more easily.', 'The hurricane had winds of more than 200 kilometers per hour.', 'The lizards eat mainly insects.'], a: 0, x: 'If toe pads grew just as much on islands without the hurricane, the hurricane probably wasn’t the cause.', w: ['', 'This explains why bigger toe pads might help, which fits the proposal.', 'Strong winds fit the proposal; they don’t weaken it.', 'Diet has nothing to do with toe pads or the hurricane.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 3, ps: 'Wildlife researchers counted the nests of two bird species in a wetland over five years. They noted that the number of nests of Species A stayed fairly stable while the number of Species B nests declined steadily, from ______', ch: { t: 'line', title: 'Nests counted in the wetland', x: ['2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023'], s: [{ n: 'Species A', v: [48, 51, 47, 50, 49] }, { n: 'Species B', v: [62, 54, 45, 37, 30] }] }, p: Q_GRAPH, fx: true, o: ['48 in 2019 to 49 in 2023.', '62 in 2019 to 30 in 2023.', '51 in 2020 to 47 in 2021.', '54 in 2020 to 62 in 2019.'], a: 1, x: 'The statement is about Species B’s decline across the whole period: 62 nests in 2019 down to 30 in 2023.', w: ['Those numbers are for Species A, which stayed stable.', '', 'Those numbers are for Species A, and they cover only one year.', 'These years are out of order and don’t describe the decline from 2019 to 2023.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'In an experiment, children were given the same puzzle. Half were told it was “very difficult”; the other half were told it was “easy.” Children who were told the puzzle was difficult kept working on it longer before giving up than children who were told it was easy, although the two groups solved the puzzle at similar rates. The researchers suggest that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['difficult puzzles are always solved faster than easy ones.', 'children’s expectations about a task’s difficulty can affect how long they persist at it.', 'telling children a puzzle is easy makes them better at solving it.', 'children cannot tell whether a puzzle is difficult.'], a: 1, x: 'The only difference was what the children were told, and it changed how long they persisted.', w: ['The two groups solved the same puzzle at similar rates, so nothing shows difficult puzzles are solved faster.', '', 'The two groups solved the puzzle at similar rates, so being told it was easy didn’t make children better.', 'The study shows what children were told mattered, not that they can’t judge difficulty.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'Tree rings from a forest show a run of unusually narrow rings for the years 1580 to 1600. Narrow rings form when trees grow very little, often because of drought. Records from a nearby farming settlement describe repeated failed harvests during the same years. Together, these findings suggest that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the settlement was founded after 1600.', 'the trees were cut down in 1600.', 'the region probably experienced drought in the late 1500s that affected both trees and crops.', 'harvests failed because the trees blocked sunlight from the fields.'], a: 2, x: 'Two independent sources point to poor growing conditions at the same time, most likely drought.', w: ['The settlement’s records cover 1580 to 1600, so it existed before 1600.', 'Nothing says the trees were cut down; the rings record their growth.', '', 'The trees were in a forest, not in the fields; the shared cause was probably drought.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The orchestra’s new conductor, Elena ______ began her first rehearsal with an unusual exercise: five minutes of silence.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['Varga', 'Varga,', 'Varga;', 'Varga:'], a: 1, x: 'The name is set off by commas after “conductor”; the second comma must close it before the verb “began”.', w: ['The name is set off by a comma after “conductor”, so it needs a closing comma.', '', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “began”.', 'A colon would cut the subject off from its verb, “began”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'Many desert animals are nocturnal. By staying underground during the day and emerging only at ______ they avoid the worst of the heat.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['night', 'night;', 'night,', 'night:'], a: 2, x: 'A long introductory phrase (“By staying… at night”) is followed by a comma.', w: ['A long introductory phrase needs a comma before the main clause.', 'A semicolon needs a complete sentence before it.', '', 'A colon needs a complete sentence before it.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The starter kit contains everything a beginning ______ paints, brushes, and a small canvas.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['artist needs:', 'artist needs;', 'artist needs,', 'artist, needs:'], a: 0, x: '“The starter kit contains everything a beginning artist needs” is complete, so a colon can introduce the list.', w: ['', 'A semicolon can’t introduce a list.', 'A comma here would make the list run on from the sentence unclearly; a colon is needed.', 'The comma between “artist” and “needs” wrongly splits the clause.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that a mold had killed the bacteria in one of his laboratory ______ discovery eventually led to penicillin, the first widely used antibiotic.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['dishes, this', 'dishes this', 'dishes; this', 'dishes, and, this'], a: 2, x: 'Two complete sentences, so a semicolon is needed. A comma alone is a comma splice.', w: ['A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', '', 'No comma belongs after “and”.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'The data from the satellite, which were collected over more than a decade, ______ that the sea ice in the region has grown steadily thinner.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['shows', 'has shown', 'is showing', 'show'], a: 3, x: '“Data” is treated as plural here (“which were collected”), so the verb is “show”.', w: ['“Data” is plural here (“which were collected”), so the verb must be plural.', '“Has shown” is singular, but “data” is plural here.', '“Is showing” is singular, but “data” is plural here.', ''] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'The committee members disagreed about the proposal, but each of them ______ to study it further before the next meeting.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['have agreed', 'has agreed', 'are agreeing', 'agree'], a: 1, x: 'The subject is “each”, which is singular, so “has agreed” is right.', w: ['“Each” is singular, so “have agreed” doesn’t agree.', '', '“Are agreeing” is plural, but “each” is singular.', '“Agree” is plural, but “each” is singular.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'The city’s new policy aims to reduce waste, ______ and to lower costs for residents.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['saving energy,', 'to save energy,', 'energy savings,', 'it saves energy,'], a: 1, x: 'The list items must match: “to reduce… to save… to lower”.', w: ['“Saving” doesn’t match “to reduce” and “to lower”.', '', 'A noun phrase doesn’t match “to reduce” and “to lower”.', 'A full clause doesn’t match “to reduce” and “to lower”.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'The cabin’s designers expected heavy snowfall every winter. ______ they built its roof at a steep angle so that snow would slide off.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Nevertheless,', 'Accordingly,', 'Instead,', 'Conversely,'], a: 1, x: 'The steep roof is a response to the expected snow, so “Accordingly” (as a result) fits.', w: ['The steep roof follows from expecting snow; it isn’t in spite of it.', '', '“Instead” signals a replacement, but the roof is a response to the snow.', '“Conversely” signals an opposite, but the roof follows from the expected snow.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Short-period comets complete an orbit around the Sun in less than 200 years. ______ long-period comets, such as Hale-Bopp, can take thousands of years to complete a single orbit.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Similarly,', 'As a result,', 'By contrast,', 'For example,'], a: 2, x: 'The two kinds of comets differ greatly, so a contrast transition fits.', w: ['Long-period comets take far longer, so they aren’t similar.', 'Long orbits aren’t a result of short-period comets’ orbits.', '', 'Long-period comets are a different group, not an example of short-period ones.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'The first version of the app packed in more than forty features. ______ users rated it poorly, complaining that it was confusing, and the company’s next version offered just five.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Accordingly,', 'Likewise,', 'Specifically,', 'Yet'], a: 3, x: 'More features might be expected to please users, but they didn’t: “Yet” signals the unexpected result.', w: ['Poor ratings aren’t what you’d expect from more features; this signals the wrong relationship.', 'Poor ratings aren’t similar to having many features.', '“Specifically” would introduce details of the features, not the users’ reaction.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- Mary Anning (1799–1847) collected fossils on the coast near Lyme Regis, England.\n- As a young woman, she found one of the first ichthyosaur skeletons to be correctly identified.\n- She later found the first complete plesiosaur skeleton.\n- Her discoveries influenced scientists’ ideas about extinction.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize the significance of Anning’s discoveries. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Mary Anning lived from 1799 to 1847.', 'Mary Anning collected fossils on the coast near Lyme Regis, England.', 'Mary Anning’s fossil discoveries, including early ichthyosaur and plesiosaur skeletons, influenced scientists’ ideas about extinction.', 'An ichthyosaur is a kind of prehistoric marine reptile.'], a: 2, x: 'Significance means why her finds mattered; only one choice says they influenced ideas about extinction.', w: ['Her life dates don’t show why her discoveries mattered.', 'Where she collected fossils doesn’t show their significance.', '', 'This defines an ichthyosaur but doesn’t mention Anning’s significance.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- Arabica and Robusta are the two most widely grown species of coffee plant.\n- Arabica is usually grown at high altitudes and is valued for its flavor.\n- Robusta grows at lower altitudes and contains more caffeine.\n- Robusta plants are more resistant to disease than Arabica plants.', p: 'The student wants to explain an advantage that Robusta has for growers compared with Arabica. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Arabica is valued for its flavor and is usually grown at high altitudes.', 'Arabica and Robusta are the two most widely grown species of coffee plant.', 'Robusta grows at lower altitudes than Arabica does.', 'Robusta plants are more resistant to disease than Arabica plants are.'], a: 3, x: 'Resistance to disease is a clear advantage for growers. Growing at lower altitudes is a difference, not necessarily an advantage.', w: ['This describes Arabica, not an advantage of Robusta.', 'This is true of both species, so it isn’t an advantage.', 'Growing at lower altitudes is a difference, not clearly an advantage for growers.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- Researchers counted bird species in 40 city parks.\n- Parks with more trees had more bird species.\n- Parks with ponds also had more bird species.\n- The number of trees predicted bird variety more strongly than the presence of a pond did.', p: 'The student wants to present the study’s main finding while also mentioning a secondary one. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Researchers counted bird species in 40 city parks.', 'Parks with ponds had more bird species than parks without ponds.', 'The study found that the number of trees in a park most strongly predicted the variety of bird species, though ponds also played a role.', 'Birds live in many city parks.'], a: 2, x: 'Only one choice gives both the main finding (trees) and the secondary one (ponds), in the right order of importance.', w: ['This describes the setup, not the findings.', 'This gives only the secondary finding.', '', 'This is too general and gives neither finding.'] }
]);
